    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d127">

                <pb facs="0212" n="108" xml:id="pg108"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123J634/210: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-01-29T21:00:00+08:00">January 29, 1932—9
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 29—6:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>146. I have just received the following telegram from the Acting Minister of
                    Foreign Affairs: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“In view of the very grave situation that has already developed in
                            Shanghai I have the honor to request Your Excellency to come to Nanking
                            at once. Please favor me with a reply. Lo Wen-kan.”</p></quote></p>
                <p>I propose to leave here for Nanking Sunday evening, January 31st, taking Engert
                    and Murray.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
